    "body": "U.S. online grocery sales rose 20.7% in the third quarter of 2026, accounting for a record 20.9% of total grocery spending, according to new research from Brick Meets Click. Delivery generated three-quarters of the increase, growing much faster than pickup.\nWhat’s Related\nThe findings draw on the firm’s monthly grocery shopper surveys for July, August and September. Online grocery sales have now grown more than 20% year over year for eight consecutive quarters.\nMore households bought groceries online, and existing customers placed orders more often. The quarterly base of monthly buyers reached almost 62% of U.S. households, up 1.2 percentage points from a year earlier, while order frequency increased nearly 14%.\nSeptember set a separate record, with nearly 64% of households buying groceries online that month. Online grocery’s rising share of spending came as in-store grocery sales declined compared with the third quarter of 2025.\nDelivery grows faster than pickup\nAll three ways of receiving online grocery orders posted sales gains, but delivery accounted for most of the growth. Its sales rose more than 30% from a year earlier, adding more than five percentage points to its share of online grocery spending.\nPickup sales grew in the mid-single digits. Ship-to-home sales also rose, though by less than the overall market’s 20.7% gain.\nThe survey distinguishes delivery from parcel shipping . Delivery includes orders brought by a retailer ’s employees or services such as Instacart and Shipt, while ship-to-home covers orders arriving through carriers and couriers such as FedEx , UPS , and USPS . Pickup includes orders collected inside or outside a store or at a designated location or locker.\n“Many of the newer eGrocery offerings focus on shorter order cycle times, while others strive to provide ways to avoid paying more than necessary,” said David Bishop, a partner at Brick Meets Click.\nShoppers use different services\nCustomers increasingly used several ways to receive their groceries within the same month. During the quarter, 17% of monthly online grocery buyers used delivery, pickup and ship-to-home, up more than five percentage points from a year earlier.\nThat overlap coincided with a rise in ordering frequency. By the end of the quarter, 81% of U.S. households had purchased groceries online at least once.\nShoppers’ stated likelihood of using the same delivery or pickup service again within 30 days reached 62%. That compares with 61% in 2025 and 56% in 2024.",
